  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nTonight through Saturday night...\n\nLingering high pressure over WI will gradually get pushed out as\na front swings in from the west associated with a surface low\npressure in north central Canada. The front will swing in to the\nwestern CWA Saturday morning with front weakening as it slides\nsoutheast with the associated upper level shortwave. This will \ncarry some precip chances through the morning and perhaps \nlingering into the afternoon. However, the best chances for \nshowers and especially storms will be focused further north. It \nis worth noting that while models and CAMs do not show robust \nconvection but the underlying conditions suggest there is at \nleast a chances for some stronger storms due to elevated\ninstability in the morning and deep layer shear around 40-45kts\nwhich is plenty to cause some severe concerns, primarily for\nlarge hail concerns given the inversion in the low levels. \n\nBy the afternoon, as things become more unstable we will lose\nmost of the good surface and upper level forcing likely making\nthe afternoon into a largely dry period. A few CAMs pick up a a\nfew showers but clearly and lack of forcing and to some degree \nmoisture becomes a bit of a concern for any chances beyond the \nmorning. There is still a chance, which will need monitoring \ngiven the good shear and instability, but the general trend \nsuggests drier. Into Saturday night there appears there may be \nadditional precip chances and even storm potential but there \nremains quite a bit of uncertainty in the forcing with some mild\nsurface convergence and perhaps some upper level weak shortwave\nenergy, the question really becomes, will that be enough. \nElevated overnight showers across northern WI are more likely \novernight.\n\nIn addition, a Beach Hazards Statement remains in effect for\nDoor County from 4PM to 1AM tonight with waves around 3-4 feet \nin the shoreline. There will be an increased risk for rip \ncurrents along the shoreline later this afternoon and evening. \n\nSunday through Friday...\n\nFocus then turns to Sunday and Monday which appear like decent\nbets to produce some storms across parts of WI in the afternoon\nand evening hours. They appear to be very similar days with very\nsimilar forcings and environments at play. Sunday appears to be\nslightly better given that it looks to have more going for it in\nterms of forcing with a strong shortwaves and decent warm front\ndraped across the CWA with increasing LLJ and WAA into the \nevening. Monday looks more focused along the front with less\nsupport but likely enough support nonetheless. The instability\nboth days will likely climb to over 3000J/kg MUCAPE with\n40-50kts of deep layer shear. Models have showed this mostly\nimpacting the eastern half of the CWA both days with more\nwestward extent Sunday. However, with all that we do know there\nlooks to be plenty of uncertainty still as we do not truly know\nexactly where this front will set up and the progression of it\ninto Monday. This will be critical because storms will very\nlikely fire right along the boundary so where this boundary is\nwill determine where the impacts for storms will be. In any case\nall hazards are possible with these events.\n\nIncreased uncertainty toward midweek but the upper levels \ngenerally show the ridge reverting west a tad with weaker high \npressure over the region and several upper level shortwaves \nrolling through. This suggests that things could very easily \nbecome active for this period despite limited potential shown in\nmodels at this time as no feature truly stands out at this \ntime.",
